    <conjecture>
      <statement>
        <p>
          For all integers <m>a,b</m> and <m>c</m>, if <m>a \mid (bc)</m>, then <m>a \mid b</m> or <m>a \mid c</m> given integers <m>a</m>, <m>b</m>, <m>c</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </conjecture>

    <p>
      Disprove this conjecture:
    </p>

    <theorem>
      <title>The Prime Divisor Theorem</title>

      <statement>
        <p>
          [The Prime Divisor Theorem]  Let <m>a</m>, <m>b</m> and <m>c</m> be natural numbers.  If <m>a \mid (bc)</m> and <m>a</m> is prime, then <m>a \mid b</m> or <m>a \mid c</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </theorem>

    <p>
      This result enables us to consider additional results on irrational numbers.
    </p>

